<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Naemul of Silla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/402">402</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Naemul was king of Silla from 356 to 402. From his reign the throne stayed with the Kim clan, and he is said to have been the first ruler to use the title maripgan. Under attack from the Wa and Gaya, Silla relied on Goguryeo, whose King Gwanggaeto sent an army to its aid in 400.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/naemul-of-silla.jpg" alt="Naemul of Silla" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JeongAhn · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RoyalTombKingNaemul061224.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Naemul, whose name can also be read Namul or Namil, was the 17th ruler of Silla. He was the son of Kim Mal-gu, a half-brother of King Michu, and of Queen Hyurye of the Kim clan, and the grandson of Kim Gudo Galmunwang. He married Michu's eldest daughter, Lady Boban. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi">Samguk sagi</a> gives him the title isageum, borne by earlier rulers, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-yusa">Samguk yusa</a> calls him maripgan, the title of later kings. Because the Chinese Book of Jin also uses the characters for maripgan when speaking of him, the title is usually thought to have begun with him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Naemul is the first Silla king named in the Book of Jin. His reign appears to have seen a great influx of Chinese culture, and the widespread use of Chinese characters seems to date from his time. In 381 he sent a tribute mission under the envoy Widu to the king of Early Jin, travelling with Goguryeo's help; asked by its ruler Fu Jian, Widu explained that the name of his country had changed to Silla.</p><p>His later years were troubled by repeated invasions by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wa-japan">Wa of Japan</a> and the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohe">Malgal</a> tribes, beginning with a massive Japanese incursion in 364 that was repulsed with great loss of life. In 392 Silla allied with Goguryeo, sending Naemul's cousin Silseong there. The Japanese besieged the capital in 393; Silla won at Doksan, probably in present-day northern Pohang, in 394, but its army was destroyed in 395.</p><p>In 400 and 401 the alliance of Goguryeo and Silla fought the allied forces of Imna and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and Silla held off Baekje only with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwanggaeto-the-great">Gwanggaeto the Great</a>. When Naemul died in 402 his sons were too young to rule, and the throne passed to Silseong, who returned from Goguryeo. One of those sons later reigned as King Nulji from 417 to 458.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Naemul of Silla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the reign of <b>Naemul</b> (r. 356–402), who used the title maripgan, the Kim held the throne by hereditary succession, and Saro gradually absorbed the other Jinhan polities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/silla">Silla · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
